{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations**\n1. For node reads, the SDK builds a `Snapshot` from the contents that `Domain::node_content` returns. Its fingerprint is `NodeContents::fingerprint` of those same contents, and it makes no further read.\n2. `Snapshot<T>` has public `content` and `fingerprint` fields and a `Snapshot::new(content, fingerprint)` constructor. It is `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n3. `Snapshot<T>` is `Clone` and `Debug` when `T` is.\n\n**Coverage**\n- **Fingerprint comes from the contents, with no extra read.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]` calls `read_snapshot` on empty and large multi-byte contents. It asserts `snapshot.content.format()` and `snapshot.fingerprint == fp(original)`. It asserts that the dependency recorded for the read has that same fingerprint. It asserts the stored reads are `[1, 1]`, one per read.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` asserts `read_snapshot(...).fingerprint == fp(\"before\")`. It asserts the reads are `[1,1,1,2]`, so `read_snapshot` adds exactly one read.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` asserts `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint` and `fingerprint.value == \"before\"`. It asserts the reads are `[1]`.\n- **The snapshot is not re-read.** The same tests change the underlying content after the read. They then check that the snapshot's content and fingerprint still show the original, and that the earlier dependency keeps `fp(\"before\")`. A re-read or later fingerprinting would show \"after\" or \"replacement\" and fail these assertions.\n- **Fingerprint under `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`.**\n  - `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks the fingerprints of reads on the real Habeas domain. The scheme and digest are exact, and they differ once the underlying source changes.\n  - The agent tests `graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery` and `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` check that the fingerprint reaches the recorded `Dependency::Node`. The Memory-graph assertion `fingerprint.value == \"x\".repeat(18_000)` is one example. The failure cases are also covered: a failed delivery records no dependency, and the prepared-versus-delivered distinction holds.\n- **Type shape.**\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint]` is a test from outside the crate. It calls `Snapshot::new` and asserts both public fields.\n  - Because that test uses `new` rather than a struct literal, it is consistent with `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n\n**Exempt or not separately tested**\n- `Clone`, `Debug` and `#[non_exhaustive]` are declarative derives and attributes on `Snapshot` in `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs`. They contain no conditional or transformation of their own. The `public_api.rs` export check also covers the item's presence.\n- This is not a case of an obligation that a value \"stays unchanged\".\n\n**Result**\nNo requirement-violating behavior in the stated obligations would pass the reviewed tests. The tests carry `{test}` markers for the subject.",
